---
import Slide from "../../components/Slide.astro"
// import Link from "../../components/Link.astro"
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro"
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'
import parametric from "../../img/maths/parametric.png"
import p1 from "../../img/maths/p1.png"
import p2 from "../../img/maths/p2.png"
import p3 from "../../img/maths/p3.png"
---
<Slide t1="Parametric Equations">
    <ul class="fragment">
        <li>Our equations so far have been of the form</li>
        \[ y = f(x) \]
    </ul>

    <ul class="fragment">
        <li>Parametric equations introduce a new independent variable
            (often written as \( t \)) and separate functions for \( x \) and \( y \)

            \[ x = f(t) \]
            \[ y = g(t) \]
        </li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="P5 Worked Examples">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">Our current examples operate over a (scaled) range
            for \( x \), calculating values
            for \( y \) which we then plot</li>
        <li class="fragment">In a parametric system, we generate a range for parameter \( t \), and calculate
            independent values for \( x \) and \( y \) which we then plot</li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Parametric Plot">
    <div class="flex justify-center items-center">
        <Image
            src={ parametric }
            alt="Parametric Plot"
            height="400"
            width="400" />
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Parametric Code">
    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="javascript" height="450px" lines="*|1,2|4-6">
            { `
const EXPR_X = "sin(t * 2.5 degrees)"
const EXPR_Y = "cos(t * 1.5 degrees)"
...
for (let t of math.range(0, 360).toArray()) {
    let x = math.evaluate(EXPR_X, {t})
    let y = math.evaluate(EXPR_Y, {t})
    
    // Map to pixel coordinates:
    let x_pixel = map(x, -1, 1, -width / 2, width / 2)
    //  Y pixels increase downwards - graphs increase upwards:
    let y_pixel = map(y, -1, 1, -height / 2, height / 2)

    circle(x_pixel, y_pixel, 5)
}
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>




<Slide t2="Variation over Time">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">We can create variations in time by working with
            P5's <code>frameCount</code> value</li>
        <li class="fragment">We use this variable alongside our existing <code>t</code>
            to change the plot as the frame count increases</li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Time-Based Code">
    <div class="text-sm-2">
        <CodeBlock lang="javascript" height="200px">
            { `
const EXPR_X = "sin((t + c) * 2.5 degrees)"
const EXPR_Y = "cos((t + c * 2) * 1.5 degrees)"
...
let c = frameCount / 50
...
for (let t of math.range(0, 360).toArray()) {
    let x = math.evaluate(EXPR_X, {t, c})
    let y = math.evaluate(EXPR_Y, {t, c})
    ...
}
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>

    <div class="columns-3">
        {
            [p1, p2, p3].map(p => (
                <div class="flex justify-center items-center">
                    <Image
                        src={ p }
                        alt="Parametric Plot"
                        height="250"
                        width="250" />
                </div>
            ))
        }
    </div>
</Slide>
